#!/usr/bin/env bash

# nixarchy:new
# omarchy:summary=Ask the default agent to do something about this machine, with the right skill already chosen.
# omarchy:args=<optimize|security|logs|gpu|disk|backup|install|update|anything> [extra...]
# omarchy:examples=nixarchy ask logs | nixarchy ask optimize

# The whole point of this file is the prompts, and the one thing they all do:
# name the skill.
#
# A small local model is bad at choosing between nine skills from their
# descriptions and fine at following one it has been handed. Naming the skill in
# the prompt moves the hard part -- routing -- from the model to this file,
# which is why the same menu row that is useful with Claude is also useful with
# a 4B model on a CPU. It is not a fallback for weak models; it makes every
# agent more reliable, because the routing is then reviewable and fixable here
# rather than re-guessed on every invocation.
#
# Every prompt also says measure-then-propose. An agent that reaches for a fix
# before it has read anything is the failure mode these tasks invite, and on a
# machine whose whole definition is one rebuild from replaced, "show me first"
# costs a few seconds and saves the afternoon.
#
# Nothing here mentions an agent by name. omarchy-agent-prompt launches whichever
# one the user chose, so these work with Claude, Codex, opencode, pi or a local
# model, and keep working when they switch.

set -uo pipefail

usage() {
  cat <<'USAGE'
Usage: nixarchy ask <task> [extra words...]

  optimize   Find what is actually slow and propose fixes
  security   Review what this machine exposes
  logs       Sweep the system state and explain what is wrong
  gpu        Work out why the GPU is not being used
  disk       Find what is filling the disk
  backup     Get the configuration into version control
  install    Install or change something, declaratively
  update     Explain what the last update changed
  anything   Type your own question

Anything after the task is appended to the prompt:

  nixarchy ask logs since yesterday
  nixarchy ask install a markdown editor
USAGE
}

task=${1:-}
[[ -z $task || $task == -h || $task == --help ]] && { usage; exit 0; }
shift
extra="$*"

if [[ -z $(omarchy-default-agent 2>/dev/null) ]]; then
  echo "No default agent is set." >&2
  echo "Choose one with: omarchy default agent <name>" >&2
  echo "Or open: Setup > Default Agent" >&2
  exit 1
fi

# Prepended to every task. Read-first and propose-don't-apply are the two rules
# that make an agent with shell access on someone's only machine reasonable.
preamble='You are working on a NixOS machine running Nixarchy. This machine is
declarative: nothing is installed with a package manager, changes are made by
editing the configuration and rebuilding, and an imperative install does not
survive the next rebuild. Read the named skill before doing anything else, and
follow it. Investigate and report what you find BEFORE changing anything, then
propose changes and wait for approval. Prefer commands that only read.'

case "$task" in
  optimize)
    prompt="$preamble

Use the nixos-performance skill. Work out what is ACTUALLY slow on this machine
before proposing anything: run the measurement block in that skill -- failed
units, PSI pressure, memory, disk, thermals, boot time -- and show me the
numbers. Then propose at most three changes, each justified by a number you
measured. Do not apply a list of tuning options that were not indicated by the
measurements."
    ;;
  security)
    prompt="$preamble

Use the nixos-security skill. Review what this machine actually exposes: start
with 'ss -tlnp' and the live firewall ruleset, and pay attention to whether each
service is bound to loopback or to the network -- that distinction answers most
of the question on its own. Then review the configuration for the common
own-goals that skill lists. Report findings by severity. Do not change the
firewall or SSH without showing me the change first: locking me out of my own
machine is the failure mode here."
    ;;
  logs)
    prompt="$preamble

Use the nixos-doctor skill. Run its sweep in order -- failed units, this boot's
errors at -p err, kernel warnings, disk, memory, and what changed in the last
generations. Quote the actual log lines you are basing conclusions on; a
paraphrased error is not evidence. Start at error level, not warning: most
warnings on a healthy Linux machine are noise, and reporting them as findings
buries the one line that matters. If everything is fine, say so plainly rather
than manufacturing findings."
    ;;
  gpu)
    prompt="$preamble

Use the nixos-gpu skill. Identify the hardware first with lspci, then work down
its three layers in order -- kernel driver, userspace runtime, application
build -- and tell me which layer is the problem before proposing a fix. Most
'GPU not working' reports are one specific layer, and skipping to the last one
is why they go unsolved. Note that a driver change needs a reboot, not just a
switch."
    ;;
  disk)
    prompt="$preamble

Use the nixos-doctor skill, the 'Disk Full' section. Find what is actually
taking the space before suggesting anything be deleted. If the Nix store is the
cause, prefer 'nix store optimise' and a dated garbage collection over
'nix-collect-garbage -d', which destroys every rollback this machine has. Ask
before deleting anything. If the space is held by one genuinely large closure,
say so rather than suggesting a garbage collection that will not help."
    ;;
  backup)
    prompt="$preamble

Use the nixos-config-repo skill. Check whether this machine's configuration is
committed and pushed anywhere. If it is not, explain what that means -- the
whole definition of this machine exists on one disk with no history -- and offer
to run 'nixarchy config repo', which does the setup properly and scans for
secrets before the first commit. Never commit a plaintext secret."
    ;;
  install)
    prompt="$preamble

Use the nixos skill. I want to install or change: ${extra:-<ask me what>}.
Pick the highest applicable route: the curated app list first, then
nixarchy-pkg-add for a plain package, then a flake edit for anything needing
options. Never install imperatively. Show me the change before rebuilding."
    ;;
  update)
    prompt="$preamble

Use the nixos and nixos-doctor skills. Tell me what changed in the most recent
system generations, using 'nix profile diff-closures --profile
/nix/var/nix/profiles/system' as the evidence rather than a changelog. If
something broke after that update, say that a rollback is one command away and
what it will and will not restore -- it restores the system, not databases or
anything else in /var."
    ;;
  anything)
    if [[ -z $extra ]]; then
      if [ -t 0 ] && command -v gum &>/dev/null; then
        extra=$(gum input --placeholder "What do you want to know about this machine?" --width 80)
        [[ -z $extra ]] && exit 0
      else
        echo "Usage: nixarchy ask anything <your question>" >&2
        exit 1
      fi
    fi
    prompt="$preamble

$extra

Pick whichever of this machine's skills fits -- nixos, nixarchy, nixos-gpu,
nixos-ai, nixos-services, nixos-secrets, nixos-performance, nixos-security,
nixos-doctor, nixos-config-repo, devenv -- and read it before answering."
    ;;
  *)
    echo "Unknown task: $task" >&2
    echo >&2
    usage >&2
    exit 1
    ;;
esac

# Appended for every task but `install`, which already consumed it, and
# `anything`, which is nothing but the extra.
if [[ -n $extra && $task != install && $task != anything ]]; then
  prompt="$prompt

Also: $extra"
fi

exec omarchy-agent-prompt "$prompt"
